Then there is also the
process reward models.

494
00:29:28,120 --> 00:29:31,200
So the difference between
process and outcome

495
00:29:31,200 --> 00:29:33,360
is that process
reward models are

496
00:29:33,360 --> 00:29:38,940
designed to give a score per
step of the generated solution.

497
00:29:38,940 --> 00:29:40,780
For example, we have
a math problems,

498
00:29:40,780 --> 00:29:43,560
there are five steps
that the model takes

499
00:29:43,560 --> 00:29:48,200
before generating a solution.

500
00:29:48,200 --> 00:29:51,560
Process reward models, again, we
learn about this more on Monday,

501
00:29:51,560 --> 00:29:56,000
I believe, are trained to
give a score for the steps

502
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:58,960
of the generation.

503
00:29:58,960 --> 00:30:02,520
So that's the difference
between the two.

504
00:30:02,520 --> 00:30:05,380
Now, for best of n
sampling with a verifier,

505
00:30:05,380 --> 00:30:06,620
again, the problem is easy.

506
00:30:06,620 --> 00:30:09,080
We generate parallel
samples, and then we

507
00:30:09,080 --> 00:30:12,120
can use an outcome-based
reward model

508
00:30:12,120 --> 00:30:15,480
to see which one has
the highest score

509
00:30:15,480 --> 00:30:20,800
and then take that
as our answer.

510
00:30:20,800 --> 00:30:26,240
Then there are other ways that
we can bring these reward models

511
00:30:26,240 --> 00:30:30,940
into the processing, into our
test time scaling framework.

512
00:30:30,940 --> 00:30:35,560
For example, here, let's assume
at each level of answering

513
00:30:35,560 --> 00:30:40,000
this question, we have a
budget of four samples.

514
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:43,080
We take these four
samples, and then here,

515
00:30:43,080 --> 00:30:49,240
use a Process Reward Model
or PRM to in this case, we

516
00:30:49,240 --> 00:30:53,380
select the top two with the
highest score from our PRM.

517
00:30:53,380 --> 00:31:00,440
Then again, we sample
from them and we continue.

518
00:31:00,440 --> 00:31:08,080
And we can have a threshold
for selecting the top two

519
00:31:08,080 --> 00:31:11,550
kind of responses in
this case and expanding

520
00:31:11,550 --> 00:31:12,570
the tree from there.

521
00:31:12,570 --> 00:31:16,030
So here, we are doing
a beam search approach

522
00:31:16,030 --> 00:31:19,030
that uses the process
reward model to guide

523
00:31:19,030 --> 00:31:21,670
how we are sampling our model.

524
00:31:21,670 --> 00:31:24,390
Is that clear?

525
00:31:24,390 --> 00:31:25,070
Yes.

526
00:31:25,070 --> 00:31:27,350
How do you choose the options?

527
00:31:27,350 --> 00:31:28,850
Based on the PRM score.

528
00:31:28,850 --> 00:31:33,230
So the process reward model
is trained to generate

529
00:31:33,230 --> 00:31:36,150
a score imagine
between 0 and 1 based

530
00:31:36,150 --> 00:31:41,730
on the step given the question,
and/or just the step alone.

531
00:31:44,600 --> 00:31:47,230
Is the PRM fine tuned for that?

532
00:31:47,230 --> 00:31:51,570
Yes, PRMs are generally also
fine tuned from language model.

533
00:31:51,570 --> 00:31:53,950
As you can imagine,
they would work better

534
00:31:53,950 --> 00:32:01,830
for in-domain types of tasks, so
if you have a certain benchmark

535
00:32:01,830 --> 00:32:04,030
that you want to optimize
for, of course, training

536
00:32:04,030 --> 00:32:07,950
a PRM on a subset of
that task helps you

537
00:32:07,950 --> 00:32:09,830
with test time
scaling on that task

538
00:32:09,830 --> 00:32:12,030
better than across new task.

539
00:32:12,030 --> 00:32:14,330
But because they're
language model based,

540
00:32:14,330 --> 00:32:17,510
they do exhibit some
generalization across tasks

541
00:32:17,510 --> 00:32:19,830
as well.

542
00:32:19,830 --> 00:32:21,270
Yes.

543
00:32:21,270 --> 00:32:24,910
For the process reward,
is this a reward

544
00:32:24,910 --> 00:32:27,210
that's being
computed in practice?

545
00:32:27,210 --> 00:32:31,970
I don't know what the research
is per token that is being done,

546
00:32:31,970 --> 00:32:34,810
or maybe blocks of tokens
and then it's being computed.

547
00:32:34,810 --> 00:32:35,310
Yes.

548
00:32:35,310 --> 00:32:36,490
It's not per token.

549
00:32:36,490 --> 00:32:39,110
It's per step.

550
00:32:39,110 --> 00:32:41,110
And this is something
we're going to learn about

551
00:32:41,110 --> 00:32:42,210
in future lectures.

552
00:32:42,210 --> 00:32:44,530
Imagine, these are
like a math problem,

553
00:32:44,530 --> 00:32:48,110
and then there are
steps to answer that.

554
00:32:48,110 --> 00:32:50,630
One way to do it is to
have humans annotate

555
00:32:50,630 --> 00:32:54,290
the quality of each step like
yes or no, this is good or bad,

556
00:32:54,290 --> 00:32:57,230
and then training the model
based on each step, which

557
00:32:57,230 --> 00:33:02,070
could be like you can assume
it's like one intellectually

558
00:33:02,070 --> 00:33:05,910
meaningful chunk of the
result, or it could be you

559
00:33:05,910 --> 00:33:08,320
